1075 #edb/ref 17592186443686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443706 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443706 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443706 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186443679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443706 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "a88b271e-3f4c-50f4-b81d-a23474066dc3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443707 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186443619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443707 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186443621 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443707 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186443638 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443707 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186443660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443707 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186443690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443707 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443707 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443707 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186443619 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443707 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "cb00ed38-5141-52cf-9136-8d37b807205f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443708 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186443628 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443708 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186443670 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443708 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186443677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443708 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186443687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443708 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186443691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443708 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443708 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443708 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186443687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443708 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f05c59ec-e7dc-5ed7-9912-91abcb45f958" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443709 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443709 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159865" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443709 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443709 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{15}$ is an I.I.D random sample where ${X}_{i}∼\\text{Bernoulli}(\\theta )$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta .$ For a particular sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{15},$ you're given that there are $6$ successes. Find the log-likelihood function of the sample.\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a Bernoulli random variable is given by* \n\n$$\nf(x)={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443709 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2725c5ee-cb05-5659-94a6-debecd1e3f50" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443709 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i};\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i};\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe are given a sample of $n=15$ independent Bernoulli random variables with unknown parameter $\\theta .$ Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\nf(x;\\theta )={\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{1-x},\\,x=0,1.\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{1-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(1-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(1-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & =S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}$ is the number of successes in the sample.\n\nFinally, since there are $6$ successes in the sample, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(15-6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +9\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443710 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186443707 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443710 #edb/ref 1069 "q-159890" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443710 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443710 #edb/ref 1071 "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},{X}_{3},{X}_{4}$ is an I.I.D random sample with ${X}_{i}∼B(4,\\theta )$ with unknown probability of success $\\theta .$ You're given the particular sample \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=1,\\,{x}_{2}=2,\\,{x}_{3}=1,\\,{x}_{4}=3.\n$$\n\nThe log-likelihood function of the sample is given by\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡a+b\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +c\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nFind the value of $a+b+c.$\n\n*Hint: The probability mass function for a binomial random variable is given by*\n\n$$\nf(x)=(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{4-x},\\,x=0,1,2,3,4.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443710 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "39034469-ded2-572d-9811-0121a992e366" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186443710 #edb/ref 1074 "Recall that if ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ are independent and identically distributed discrete random variables with probability mass function $f(x;\\theta ),$ where $\\theta $ is an unknown parameter, then the log-likelihood function of a random sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡L(\\theta ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}f({x}_{i},\\theta )) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(f({x}_{i},\\theta )).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn our case, we are given a sample of $n=4$ independent binomial random variables with an unknown probability of success $\\theta $ and $N=4$ as the number of trials. Therefore, the probability mass function is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,\\theta ) & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{N}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{N-x},\\,x=0,1,…,N \\\\  & =(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{x}){\\theta }^{x}(1-\\theta )^{4-x},\\,x=0,1,2,3,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can calculate the log-likelihood function as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}l(\\theta ) & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡((\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}}){\\theta }^{{x}_{i}}(1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\theta }^{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡((1-\\theta )^{4-{x}_{i}}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})+{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(4-{x}_{i})\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta {\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ){\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}(4-{x}_{i}) \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(4n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $S={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}.$\n\nNow, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{1}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{2}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{3}})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{4}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}})).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, our expression for $l(\\theta )$ becomes\n\n$$\nl(\\theta )=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({\\prod }_{i=1}^{n}(\\genfrac{}{}{0pt}{}{4}{{x}_{i}}))+S\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\theta +(4n-S)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1-\\theta ).\n$$\n\nNow, 